steps.body:
To walk backwards, count down from the last index to 0 with a signed `int`:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    {{code}}
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
for (int i = static_cast<int>(v.size()) - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
    std::cout << ' ' << v[i];
}
```

Two traps make the cast necessary. The first is the one the last lesson warned about: with `std::size_t i`, the test `i >= 0` is *always* true, because an unsigned value is never negative. When `i` is 0, `--i` wraps around to 18446744073709551615 and the loop reads far outside the vector, which is undefined behavior. And `v.size() - 1` on an empty vector wraps the same way, since `v.size()` is unsigned. Converting the size to `int` before subtracting makes the start index -1 for an empty vector, so the loop never runs.

Printing backwards leaves the vector unchanged. To reverse it in place, swap each element in the first half with its mirror:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77};
    {{code}}
    for (int x : v) {
        std::cout << x << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
const std::size_t n = v.size();
for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n / 2; ++i) {
    std::swap(v[i], v[n - 1 - i]);
}
```

Stop at `n / 2`, or the second half swaps everything back. With an odd count the middle element stays put. No second vector is needed: values trade places inside the same heap block.

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> levels = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    std::cout << "Backwards:";
    for (int i = static_cast<int>(levels.size()) - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
        std::cout << ' ' << levels[i];
    }
    std::cout << '\n';

    std::vector<int> ids = {11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77};
    const std::size_t n = ids.size();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n / 2; ++i) {
        std::swap(ids[i], ids[n - 1 - i]);
    }
    std::cout << "Reversed:";
    for (int id : ids) {
        std::cout << ' ' << id;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}

