steps.body:
Some loops look at pairs or groups of elements instead of one at a time.

**Neighbors.** Start at 1 so `v[i - 1]` always exists:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> v = {5, 3, 8, 2, 9};
    int count = 0;
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "count = " << count << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
for (std::size_t i = 1; i < v.size(); ++i) {
    if (v[i] > v[i - 1]) {
        ++count;
    }
}
```

This is safe on an empty vector too, where `1 < 0` is false. Comparing `v[i]` with `v[i + 1]` under `i < v.size() - 1` is not: on an empty vector `v.size() - 1` wraps to a huge number and the loop reads past the end.

**Prefix sums.** Keep a running total and push each value of it into a new vector. `totals[i]` holds the sum of `v[0]` through `v[i]`. If totals could pass about 2 billion, use `long long` instead of `int`.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> v = {5, 3, 8, 2, 9};
    {{code}}
    for (int t : totals) {
        std::cout << t << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::vector<int> totals;
int running = 0;
for (int x : v) {
    running += x;
    totals.push_back(running);
}
```

**Sliding windows.** For the best sum of `K` elements in a row, add up the first window once, then slide it one step at a time: add the element entering and subtract the one leaving.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> v = {5, 3, 8, 2, 9};
    const std::size_t K = 2;
    int windowSum = v[0] + v[1];  // the first window
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "last window sum = " << windowSum << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
for (std::size_t i = K; i < v.size(); ++i) {
    windowSum += v[i] - v[i - K];
}
```

Each slide costs one addition and one subtraction instead of re-adding all `K` values. The vector needs at least `K` elements, or the first window reads past the end.

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> sales = {5, 3, 8, 2, 9};

    int betterDays = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < sales.size(); ++i) {
        if (sales[i] > sales[i - 1]) {
            ++betterDays;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "Better days: " << betterDays << '\n';

    std::vector<int> totals;
    int running = 0;
    for (int s : sales) {
        running += s;
        totals.push_back(running);
    }
    std::cout << "Running totals:";
    for (int t : totals) {
        std::cout << ' ' << t;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';

    const std::size_t WINDOW_SIZE = 2;
    int windowSum = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < WINDOW_SIZE; ++i) {
        windowSum += sales[i];
    }
    int best = windowSum;
    for (std::size_t i = WINDOW_SIZE; i < sales.size(); ++i) {
        windowSum += sales[i] - sales[i - WINDOW_SIZE];
        if (windowSum > best) {
            best = windowSum;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "Best 2-day sum: " << best << '\n';
    return 0;
}

